# AWS Cleanup Workflow

- Always explain implementation approach before code changes.
- One module per AWS billing service (ELB, EC2, VPC, S3, CloudFront, KMS, ...).
- Elastic IP and NAT belong to VPC, not EC2. Load balancers belong to ELB, not EC2.
- Mark new modules as `PENDING_SECONDARY_VERIFICATION`.
- Keep each module small. Shared helpers live in `lib/common.sh`.
- Cloud Shell entry is one command: bootstrap.sh runs all modules then leftover verify.
- After cleanup, verify leftovers with all-region Describe APIs (EC2 Global View style).
- Cloud Shell default one-liner does not write S3 reports. `--report-s3` publishes a public-read FAIL report to `aws-cleanup-report-<account-id>`. PASS always deletes that bucket.
- Delete order: IAM users first (freeze); CloudTrail/Config/EventBridge/CloudWatch before S3; EC2 before VPC; CloudFront before S3; KMS after data; IAM roles last.
- IAM users and roles are split: users at start, roles at end. Skip only the current caller identity and service-linked roles.
- Route53: keep only apex NS/SOA; disable DNSSEC and Accelerated Recovery; poll then delete.
- RDS/Redshift/DynamoDB: disable deletion protection, delete the database, then delete snapshots.
- S3: log bucket i/total, batch size, elapsed time, 15s heartbeat. Do not recommend tmux.
- AWS Backup: EFS automatic vault Denies DeleteRecoveryPoint and DeleteBackupVaultAccessPolicy. Put Allow policy first, then delete recovery points. Do not disassociate. Skip undeletable aws/efs/automatic-backup-vault after empty.
- WorkMail: deregister users/groups/resources, then force-delete organization (do not delete Directory Service).
- CloudFront: cancel the flat-rate plan (that switches it to pay-as-you-go) before disable. Free plans cancel immediately. Pro/Business/Premium take effect at the end of the billing period; skip those distributions until a later run. Delete tenants first, detach continuous deployment, disable, wait until Deployed, then delete. Leave bundled WAF, Route53, and KeyValueStore in place.
